  <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6 mb-8">
    <h2>1. The Anatomy of a High-Performance Door Sill Pan</h2>
    <p>
      Under building envelope science (ASTM E2112), you must assume that all exterior door perimeters will eventually admit water. Whether through hairline thermal caulk cracks, driving winds, or worn weatherstripping, water will pass behind the outer door threshold. A proper sill pan consists of four critical anatomical components:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1. Sloped Floor Bed:</strong> A flat or slightly sloped floor that pitches at least 1/8" per foot toward the exterior.</li>
      <li><strong>2. Interior Rear Back-Dam:</strong> A vertical upturn along the inside edge of the subfloor (at least 3/8" to 1/2" high). This mechanical dam prevents water driven by 50+ mph wind pressure from splashing over into interior hardwood or carpet.</li>
      <li><strong>3. Vertical End-Dams (Dog Ears):</strong> Turned-up vertical flanges climbing at least 2 inches (preferably 4 inches) up the framing rough opening side jambs. These prevent lateral capillary water spread into the jack studs.</li>
      <li><strong>4. Exterior Drainage Apron:</strong> A downturned front lip that laps shingle-fashion over the exterior weather-resistant barrier (WRB / Tyvek) and foundation flashing, directing water harmlessly outside.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Rigid PVC Pan vs. Flexible Self-Adhering Butyl Membrane</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Feature</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Rigid PVC 3-Piece Kit (SureSill / Jamsill)</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Flexible Butyl Membrane (DuPont FlexWrap)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Material Composition</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Extruded rigid PVC with PVC solvent-weld seams</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">100% butyl adhesive with creped polyolefin backing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Corner Seam Integrity</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Molded factory corners (Zero potential leaks)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Seamless single-sheet stretch around corners</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Built-in Back Dam</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Yes: Molded rigid 1/2" rear barrier lip</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-amber-700 font-bold">Requires wood bevel strip or custom back-dam curb</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Durability Under Heavy Doors</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Extremely high (Crush-proof under 200 lb slabs)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Self-seals around fasteners, but flexible</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">DIY Installation Ease</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">High (Cut to width, glue center splice)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Moderate (Requires skilled stretching &amp; rolling)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. The "Weep Gap" Caulk Rule: Don't Trap Water!</h2>
    <p>
      The single most devastating mistake contractors make when installing sill pans is caulking the front exterior apron completely shut. If you run a solid bead of sealant along the front edge of the door sill, you create a bathtub! Water that enters around the side jambs drains into the pan, reaches the front lip, hits the solid caulk dam, pools upward, overflows the rear back dam, and floods the subfloor!
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Proper Three-Bead Sealant Strategy:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Back Bead:</strong> Run a continuous heavy bead of polyurethane sealant along the top inside edge of the rear back-dam.</li>
      <li><strong>Side Beads:</strong> Run continuous beads up the vertical side end-dams.</li>
      <li><strong>Front Bed (Broken Weep Holes):</strong> Place 2-inch beads of caulk under the exterior sill, leaving <strong>1-inch open uncaulked weep gaps</strong> every 12 inches. Any water entering the pan can escape freely to the outside world.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Trade Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    expertName="David Miller, PE"
    role="Senior Structural & Geotechnical Engineer"
    experience="25+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Why Modern High-Performance Flexible Flashing Requires a Hand Pressure Roller"
    note="When you use flexible stretchable flashing like DuPont FlexWrap or Huber ZIP System liquid flash, DIYers often press the tape into the corners with their thumbs and assume it's stuck. Self-adhering butyl adhesives are 'pressure-sensitive adhesives' (PSA). At the molecular level, butyl requires a minimum of 15 to 20 PSI of mechanical force to wet out the microscopic pores in the OSB or plywood subfloor! If you only press with your thumbs, the tape develops micro-channels where capillary water creeps underneath. Always buy an inexpensive 2-inch hard rubber or steel J-roller. Lean your body weight onto the roller and roll every square inch of the sill pan, especially the 90-degree corner transitions where the pan turns up the framing studs."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Door Sill Pan Flashing"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Fastening Screws Through the Horizontal Floor of the Sill Pan",
        costImpact: "$3,800 (Punctured Water Barrier & Concealed Subfloor Rot)",
        description: "Driving threshold anchor screws directly down through the horizontal base of the pan creates penetration holes at the lowest water collection point.",
        solution: "Never screw through the horizontal bottom of a sill pan. Anchor the door frame through the vertical side jambs, or use pre-molded screw boss channels with EPDM gaskets."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Omitting the Rear Interior Back-Dam Lip",
        costImpact: "$2,600 (Wind-Driven Water Spilling Over Subfloor Edge)",
        description: "Laying a flat piece of flashing without a vertical rear lip allows wind-driven rain during thunderstorms to blow straight across the pan into the home.",
        solution: "Ensure a minimum 3/8\" to 1/2\" vertical back dam along the rear edge, or bevel a cedar strip under the flashing to create a positive barrier."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Tucking the Front Flashing Apron BEHIND the Housewrap",
        costImpact: "$4,200 (Water Funneled Directly Behind Siding & Rim Joists)",
        description: "Reversing the shingle-lap order funnels drainage water directly behind the building's weather-resistant barrier onto the rim joist.",
        solution: "Strictly adhere to weatherboard shingle-lapping: the sill pan apron must ALWAYS lap OVER the exterior housewrap and foundation flashing."
      }
    ]}
  />

